  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Why does a tokamak need such strong magnets?",
      "a": "Plasma at 150 million degrees would instantly cool and damage any wall it touched. Plasma is made of charged particles, which spiral along magnetic field lines, so strong magnets can trap it in a ring without touching anything.",
      "asks": [
        "How do magnets hold the plasma?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is plasma?",
      "a": "Plasma is often called the fourth state of matter: a gas so hot that electrons are torn off the atoms, leaving a mix of charged particles. Lightning, neon signs and the Sun are all plasma.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the fourth state of matter?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What fuel does fusion use?",
      "a": "Most designs use deuterium and tritium, two heavy forms of hydrogen. Deuterium can be taken from seawater, while tritium is rare and would be made inside the reactor's blanket from lithium.",
      "asks": [
        "Where does fusion fuel come from?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How is the plasma heated to 150 million degrees?",
      "a": "A current driven through the plasma heats it like a kettle element, then beams of fast particles and powerful radio waves add much more heat, a bit like a microwave oven heating food.",
      "asks": [
        "How do you heat a plasma?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the blanket do?",
      "a": "The blanket lines the chamber and catches the fast neutrons, turning their energy into heat that could make steam. Blankets containing lithium would also breed new tritium fuel.",
      "asks": [
        "How would a fusion power station make electricity?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "In 2023 the JET tokamak in England produced a record 69 megajoules of fusion energy in a single pulse of about 5 seconds.",
    "ITER, a large international tokamak being built in France, is designed to make ten times more fusion power than the heating power put into its plasma."
  ],
